#N canvas 20 20 7000 7270 12;
#X text 20 20 debug-bench -- the debug patch acceptance run. IT STOPS THE INSTRUMENT -- selecting any patch restarts Pd \, which is exactly why this tool exists and exactly why it is not for mid-set. MOST OF IT IS PROVEN HEADLESSLY: test/gate/debug-assert.sh asserts all five screen rows \, the channel-block decode and both probes in about thirteen seconds. What is left here is what only hardware can show -- that the patch WIRED ITSELF \, which is step 3 and the one everything else rests on. STEPPED BY HAND: press GO to run the step that has just been described \, press GO again to describe the next one. Nothing moves until you ask it to \, so the PASS IF is on screen and STILL while you read it. The prompt line always says what the next press will do., f 120;
#X text 20 150 GO ON THE MAC: the bng below \, or the dev panel's ENCODER CLICK -- u_mother-stub sends encbut unconditionally and nothing in Cut It consumes it. TURNING the encoder repeats the current step without advancing \, which is what to do when you looked away., f 120;
#X text 2400 200 ⚠️ GO ON THE DEVICE IS NOT THE ENCODER -- IT IS netcat \, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS NOT OPTIONAL. mother only forwards encbut once a patch has banged enableSubMenu \, and NOTHING IN Cut It EVER ASKS -- m_organelle leaves the encoder out on purpose. So the click that drives this bench on the Mac is silently dead on the Organelle. Send instead with ./test/run.sh --bench <name> from the Mac. ⚠️ NOT netcat: the line this file used to print does nothing on macOS -- BSD nc exits before the datagram is flushed at -w0 \, and -w1 was measured to fail too \, while the port IS bound and the bench is fine. It looks exactly like a dead bench. The device cannot send to itself either: busybox here has no nc at all. Asking mother to enable the encoder is NOT the fix either: it is an OVERRIDE that takes the turn AND the click \, so the bench would eat the press that leaves the patch. Items 313 and 314 \, ref/device/organelle.md., f 120;
#X text 20 280 Load it as a THIRD patch after mother.pd and main.pd. It touches nothing in the deployed patch -- it only pushes onto the same buses a controller would. A PASS IF is printed for EVERY step including the ones whose correct result is that nothing happens \, which are otherwise impossible to mark off., f 120;
#X text 2400 20 ON THE MAC EVERY ERROR STEP ALSO PRINTS /sdcard/cut-it-err.cur: write failed \, AND THAT IS NOT A FAILED STEP. u_err keeps its durable log on the Organelle's SD card \, which no Mac has -- so the write is attempted and refused every time. It is in fact the only proof off-device that the logging path ran at all. On the device that line must NOT appear \, and if it does the card is unwritable and the log is being lost silently., f 120;
#X obj 6000 40 r \$0-say;
#X obj 6000 100 print;
#X text 20 360 GO -- click here \, or the dev panel's encoder button. MAC ONLY -- on the device use netcat \, top right., f 60;
#X obj 20 420 bng 40 250 50 0 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -262144 -1 -1;
#X obj 20 500 s \$0-go;
#X obj 300 420 r encbut;
#X obj 300 480 select 1;
#X obj 300 540 t b;
#X obj 300 600 s \$0-go;
#X text 560 480 encbut sends 1 THEN 0 \, so select 1 is what stops one press counting twice., f 46;
#X obj 300 680 netreceive 9998 1;
#X obj 300 740 route go rerun where show;
#X obj 300 800 t b;
#X obj 300 860 s \$0-go;
#X obj 620 800 t b;
#X obj 620 860 s \$0-rerun;
#X text 860 800 rerun fires the CURRENT step again without advancing \, so a visual step can be looked at more than once. [r]epeat in test/run.sh sends it., f 46;
#X obj 1500 800 t b b;
#X obj 1680 860 f;
#X obj 1500 860 f;
#X obj 1500 920 pack f f;
#X obj 1500 980 print bench-at;
#X obj 1500 740 r \$0-set-step;
#X obj 1680 740 r \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 2000 800 t b;
#X obj 2000 860 s \$0-repeat;
#X text 2200 800 show goes through $0-repeat \, which is the encoder's path too -- one way to re-describe a step \, not two., f 46;
#X obj 1400 420 r enc;
#X obj 1400 480 t b;
#X obj 1400 540 s \$0-repeat;
#X text 1660 480 enc is 1 for up and 0 for down -- not plus or minus one -- so either direction repeats., f 46;
#X text 20 920 THE STATE MACHINE. phase 0 means the step has been described and not yet run \; phase 1 means it has run. SETTING THE STEP NUMBER IS WHAT DESCRIBES IT \, so advancing is just an increment -- there is no second path to keep in step., f 120;
#X obj 20 980 r \$0-go;
#X obj 20 1040 f;
#X obj 20 1100 select 0 1;
#X obj 300 1040 r \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 20 1170 t b b;
#X msg 300 1230 1;
#X obj 300 1290 s \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 20 1290 f;
#X obj 20 1350 s \$0-do-run;
#X obj 20 1230 r \$0-rerun;
#X obj 900 1170 t b b;
#X msg 1180 1230 0;
#X obj 1180 1290 s \$0-set-phase;
#X obj 900 1350 f;
#X obj 900 1410 + 1;
#X obj 900 1470 s \$0-set-step;
#X obj 1700 980 r \$0-set-step;
#X obj 1700 1040 t f f f;
#X obj 1700 1110 s \$0-set-inc;
#X obj 1980 1110 s \$0-set-run;
#X obj 1180 1350 r \$0-set-inc;
#X obj 300 1410 r \$0-set-run;
#X obj 2260 1110 s \$0-do-show;
#X obj 2600 980 r \$0-repeat;
#X obj 2600 1040 f;
#X obj 2780 980 r \$0-set-run;
#X obj 2600 1100 s \$0-do-show;
#X obj 3100 980 loadbang;
#X obj 3100 1040 del 500;
#X msg 3100 1100 1;
#X obj 3100 1160 s \$0-set-step;
#X text 20 1920 DESCRIBING A STEP. One select per step rather than one wide fan \, so every cord stays short and local -- the same reason u_map chains its routes., f 120;
#X obj 20 2020 r \$0-do-show;
#X obj 20 2080 select 1;
#X obj 240 2080 t b b b;
#X msg 700 2080 === STEP-01-of-09 === It is in the menu -- under debug and not under the instrument;
#X msg 700 2130 PASS IF: The Organelle menu has a debug folder beside the one holding Cut It -- and Cut It Debug is inside it. Finding it in the same folder as the instrument is the failure. At a venue you should scroll past nothing to reach what you play.;
#X msg 700 2180 >>> press GO to run step 1 of 9;
#X obj 240 2250 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 2340 select 2;
#X obj 240 2340 t b b b;
#X msg 700 2340 === STEP-02-of-09 === It loads and comes up on the MIDI screen;
#X msg 700 2390 PASS IF: Within a couple of seconds the screen reads 1-MIDI-IN with four rows under it -- lp nano sp404 and ch -- all reading zero. A screen that stays on the menu or goes blank is the failure.;
#X msg 700 2440 >>> press GO to run step 2 of 9;
#X obj 240 2510 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 2600 select 3;
#X obj 240 2600 t b b b;
#X msg 700 2600 === STEP-03-of-09 === It wired itself -- and proving that is the whole bench;
#X msg 700 2650 PASS IF: Moving a nanoKONTROL fader makes the nano row count up. This is the step everything else rests on: loading any patch drops the ALSA connections and the patch runs wire.sh itself to put them back. A nano row stuck on zero means the patch is measuring silence and would report a healthy rig as dead.;
#X msg 700 2700 >>> press GO to run step 3 of 9;
#X obj 240 2770 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 2860 select 4;
#X obj 240 2860 t b b b;
#X msg 700 2860 === STEP-04-of-09 === Each device lands on its own row;
#X msg 700 2910 PASS IF: A Launchpad pad moves only the lp row. An SP-404 pad moves only the sp404 row. Each one also changes the ch row to a number in that device's own block -- ch 1 to 16 for the Launchpad and 33 to 42 for the 404 bank. Two devices sharing one row is the fault this patch exists to find.;
#X msg 700 2960 >>> press GO to run step 4 of 9;
#X obj 240 3030 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3120 select 5;
#X obj 240 3120 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3120 === STEP-05-of-09 === The error log screen shows what the instrument left behind;
#X msg 700 3170 PASS IF: The screen reads 3-ERR-LOG and the four rows under it hold the last four lines of the instrument s own log with dots where the spaces were. Four dashes mean the log was empty or missing -- which is a real answer and not a failure if the instrument has not raised anything.;
#X msg 700 3220 >>> press GO to run step 5 of 9;
#X obj 240 3290 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3380 select 6;
#X obj 240 3380 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3380 === STEP-06-of-09 === The network screen shows the address you type into the phone;
#X msg 700 3430 PASS IF: The screen reads 4-NETWORK and the row under it reads ip- followed by this Organelle's own address. That is the number PdParty needs -- it will not resolve the name -- and reading it here is what removes the last reason this rig needs a laptop to fix the phone link.;
#X msg 700 3480 >>> press GO to run step 6 of 9;
#X obj 240 3550 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3640 select 7;
#X obj 240 3640 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3640 === STEP-07-of-09 === Both output devices answer when you fire at them;
#X msg 700 3690 PASS IF: The C an octave up plays a note on the Volca. The D above it plays pad A1 on the SP-404. The sent counter goes up by exactly one per press -- two for one finger would mean a key release is firing as well as the press.;
#X msg 700 3740 >>> press GO to run step 7 of 9;
#X obj 240 3810 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 3900 select 8;
#X obj 240 3900 t b b b;
#X msg 700 3900 === STEP-08-of-09 === A re-wire by hand reports how many links it made;
#X msg 700 3950 PASS IF: The re-wire screen reads 5-RE-WIRE and the links row shows a number rather than a dash -- nine on the full rig. The runs row goes up by one each time you press the key. A links row still reading dash means wire.sh ran but said nothing back.;
#X msg 700 4000 >>> press GO to run step 8 of 9;
#X obj 240 4070 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4160 select 9;
#X obj 240 4160 t b b b;
#X msg 700 4160 === STEP-09-of-09 === You can get out of it -- and the instrument comes back;
#X msg 700 4210 PASS IF: The lowest key returns you to the Organelle menu. Cut It then loads and runs normally -- and when you leave it the Launchpad goes back to Live Mode with its Setup button responding. A Launchpad still dark and ignoring Setup is the failure.;
#X msg 700 4260 >>> press GO to run step 9 of 9;
#X obj 240 4330 s \$0-say;
#X obj 20 4420 t b;
#X msg 240 4420 === BENCH COMPLETE === every step has been run -- reload the patch to go round again;
#X obj 240 4480 s \$0-say;
#X text 20 4560 RUNNING A STEP. The actions fire in the order they are listed -- rightmost trigger outlet first -- and the line saying the step has fired goes out LAST \, off outlet 0 \, so it cannot arrive before the thing it describes., f 120;
#X obj 20 4660 r \$0-do-run;
